"titre2": "Exercise 3",
"texte": [
"Critical Intellectual Dialogue — Characters: Eleanor, a sociologist of stratification; Marcus, an economist and theorist of digital capitalism — Marcus: 'Let us give hypermodernity its due, Eleanor: over the past half-century, the rigid hereditary castes, aristocratic titles, and overt legal exclusions that defined industrial and pre-industrial societies have largely crumbled across the democratic world. Today, social status is no longer fixed at birth by bloodline or landownership; it circulates through open educational competition, entrepreneurial innovation, and global talent markets. Surely this represents a historic democratisation of social mobility rather than a mere perpetuation of old hierarchies.'",
"Eleanor: 'Your optimism mistakes a metamorphosis of hierarchy for its abolition, Marcus. Hypermodernity has indeed dismantled many visible barriers of aristocratic birth, only to erect subtler, more pervasive, and far harder-to-contest forms of stratification. Where nineteenth-century class divisions were starkly legible in factory gates, legal privileges, and inherited estates, contemporary hierarchies operate through asymmetric control over cognitive capital, algorithmic visibility, spatial mobility, and temporal autonomy. The new ruling strata do not need titles of nobility when they possess global passports, elite educational credentials, and ownership of digital infrastructures.'",
"Marcus: 'Yet educational credentials and technological entrepreneurship remain, at least in principle, open to talent regardless of background. When a software engineer or a researcher rises from modest origins to global prominence, does that not confirm that meritocratic competence has replaced inherited privilege as the primary engine of social ranking?'",
"Eleanor: 'Exceptions do not invalidate a structural rule; on the contrary, isolated success stories often serve as the most persuasive alibi for systemic inequality. What hypermodernity celebrates as pure meritocracy frequently conceals an unprecedented accumulation of invisible advantages: early cognitive stimulation, private tutoring, international internships, cultural fluency, and social networks that convert economic wealth into ostensibly objective academic merit. Worse still, as the philosopher Michael Sandel has incisively argued, meritocratic stratification inflicts a deeper moral wound than aristocratic hierarchy: those at the top believe they wholly deserve their triumph, while those left behind are invited to view their marginalisation as personal failure.'",
"Marcus: 'I grant you the moral arrogance of meritocratic hubris, Eleanor, and I would add a distinctly economic dimension that distinguishes hypermodernity from earlier capitalism: winner-take-all network dynamics. In digitalised global markets, tiny differences in ranking, platform centrality, or algorithmic amplification translate into astronomical disparities in income and influence. Meanwhile, the labour market itself polarises between a hyper-mobile cognitive elite manipulating symbols and capital, and a vast precariat performing algorithmic gig work or essential care services under relentless surveillance.'",
"Eleanor: 'Precisely—and notice how hypermodernity even stratifies our relationship to time and attention. Today, genuine offline privacy, unhurried reflection, human-to-human healthcare, and unmediated education have become luxury goods reserved for the affluent, whereas the majority are relegated to automated interfaces, algorithmic nudging, and fragmented attention. Hierarchy is no longer merely about how much money one possesses; it is about whose time is sovereign and whose life is continuously monitored and optimised by others.'",
"Marcus: 'If neither aristocratic tradition nor unbridled market meritocracy produces a just order, then reshaping social hierarchies in a humane direction requires rethinking what society values and rewards in the first place.'",
"Eleanor: 'Indeed: dismantling hypermodern stratification means revaluing essential care and manual labour, taxing concentrated digital and financial rents, democratising access to knowledge and unmediated public services, and reminding ourselves that equal human dignity must never be held hostage to one's position in an algorithmic or economic ranking.'"
