    "texte": [
      "Philosophical and Ethical Essay — Born largely as a generous intellectual antidote to imperial arrogance, ethnocentrism, and missionary dogmatism, moral relativism insists that ethical norms possess no validity outside the specific historical, linguistic, or cultural communities that forge them. On this view, judging another society's customs by external standards is not merely an error of perspective, but an act of cultural imperialism. Yet while this sensitivity to human plurality rightly dismantled the colonial delusion that one civilisation held a monopoly on virtue, radical moral relativism encounters a formidable crisis the moment it confronts extreme cruelty, systemic oppression, or crimes against humanity. When torture, slavery, the subjugation of women, or the persecution of minorities is defended under the banner of local tradition or cultural authenticity, relativism finds itself disarmed—unable to condemn atrocity without contradicting its own foundational premise.",
      "The first logical flaw of radical relativism lies in its self-refuting absolutism. To assert as an unconditional rule that 'no moral principle is universally valid across cultures' is already to smuggle in a universal norm—namely, that all societies ought to tolerate and refrain from judging one another's practices. Yet tolerance itself is not a neutral void; it is a substantive moral value rooted in the recognition of equal human dignity. If a culture repudiates tolerance and practices violent domination over its own vulnerable members or neighbours, the consistent relativist has no coherent grounds on which to object, for doing so would require appealing to a cross-cultural standard of justice that relativism denies exists.",
      "Equally problematic is the sociological fiction upon which cultural relativism quietly depends: the assumption that cultures are monolithic, harmonious, and static wholes speaking with a single unanimous voice. In reality, every human society—past and present, Western and non-Western alike—is traversed by internal dissent, moral reform movements, and courageous dissidents who contest inherited injustices from within. When external observers invoke 'cultural relativism' to excuse authoritarian repression or patriarchal violence in another society, they do not respect that culture; rather, they side selectively with its most powerful gatekeepers while silencing the voices of its own internal victims and reformers.",
      "The bitter irony of uncritical relativism is that it often ends up mirroring the very paternalism it claims to oppose. Whereas old imperialists declared other peoples incapable of civilisation without foreign tutelage, radical relativists imply that certain populations are somehow indifferent to bodily pain, arbitrary imprisonment, humiliation, or silencing—as though the aspiration to dignity and freedom were an eccentric local quirk rather than a deep anthropological constant. Across every continent and historical epoch, human beings may disagree richly over dietary laws, family rituals, or civic etiquette, yet they exhibit a remarkably convergent recoil against gratuitous cruelty, betrayal, and tyrannical humiliation.",
      "Nevertheless, rejecting moral relativism must never become an excuse for reviving an arrogant, homogeneous universalism that imposes one society's institutional blueprint upon the entire planet by economic coercion or military force. History offers ample warning of how noble phrases like 'universal human rights' have been cynically instrumentalised by great powers to justify selective interventions while overlooking their own abuses. Genuine moral universalism is neither a uniform legal code dictated from a single centre nor a erasure of cultural richness; it is what the philosopher Michael Walzer termed a 'thin' universal core of fundamental protections—against murder, enslavement, torture, and persecution—embedded within 'thick', diverse cultural languages and local traditions.",
      "Building a credible cross-cultural ethic therefore requires replacing both relativistic indifference and imperial imposition with patient dialogical universalism. As thinkers such as Kwame Anthony Appiah and Martha Nussbaum have argued, universal values gain authentic legitimacy not when proclaimed unilaterally, but when discovered and enriched through reciprocal conversation among equals—a process in which every tradition brings its own moral resources to bear upon shared human vulnerabilities.",
      "Ultimately, moral relativism fails the test of human solidarity because suffering speaks a language that requires no cultural translation. Respecting the diversity of human ways of life is an essential virtue, yet diversity ceases to be a moral good the instant it becomes an alibi for cruelty. True ethical maturity lies in holding two truths together without flinching: that humanity flourishes in a splendid plurality of forms, and that every human being, everywhere, possesses an inviolable dignity that no custom, border, or ideology has the right to trample."
